<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chlodomer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 495–<a href="/years/524">524</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Chlodomer of Orléans, Clodomir · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chlodomer was a Frankish king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a>, the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> and Clotilde. When his father's realm was divided in 511 he received lands along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>. He was killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vezeronce">Battle of Vézeronce</a> in 524, after which his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/childebert-i">Childebert I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a> murdered two of his young sons and divided his kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chlodomer.jpg" alt="Chlodomer" width="269" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: schiesser philippe, Eric Vandenbossche · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chlodomer_denarius.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chlodomer, also spelled Clodomir or Clodomer, was one of the four sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> and his wife Clotilde. On his father's death in 511 he divided the kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> with his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theuderic-i">Theuderic I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/childebert-i">Childebert I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a>, and became king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>. His kingdom included the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> and Orléans. He married Guntheuc, and they had three sons: Theodebald, Gunthar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clodoald">Clodoald</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 523 and 524 Chlodomer and his brothers invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, possibly at the instigation of his mother Clotilde, who wished to avenge the death of her parents, allegedly murdered by her uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gundobad">Gundobad</a>, the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-of-burgundy">Sigismund of Burgundy</a>. Gregory of Tours tells this story of Clotilde's revenge, but it is assumed to be apocryphal. After capturing Sigismund, Chlodomer returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> and had Sigismund and his sons Gisald and Gondebaud killed in May 524. Sigismund's brother Gondomar then returned in triumph at the head of troops sent by his ally, the Ostrogothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a>, and massacred the garrison the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> had left behind. Chlodomer led a second expedition and was killed in June 524 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vezeronce">Battle of Vézeronce</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his death his kingdom was divided: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a> and Poitou, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/childebert-i">Childebert I</a> the lands on both banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>. His widow married Chlothar, and Chlodomer's three sons were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and entrusted to their grandmother Clotilde. Two of them, Theodebald, aged ten, and Gunthar, aged seven, were murdered so that they would not claim a share of their father's inheritance. Only the youngest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clodoald">Clodoald</a>, was saved by a few loyal followers. Better known as Saint Cloud, he gave up his hair, the symbol of Frankish royalty, rather than his life, and later became abbot of Nogent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chlodomer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the division of 511 Clovis' second son <b>Chlodomer</b> received lands along the Loire, with Orléans as his capital, together with Tours, Bourges and part of Aquitaine.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-orleans">Kingdom of Orléans</a></li><li><blockquote>He joined his brother Chlothar I in murdering their nephews, the sons of <b>Chlodomer of Orléans</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-paris">Kingdom of Paris</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Vézeronce-Curtin, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.65,5.47&amp;year=513" aria-label="Vézeronce-Curtin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Chlodomer"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5327 6201h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5327 6201h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=45.65,5.47&amp;year=513">What was here in 513 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
